    description: "WHAT. linkers/type_hierarchy matches an overriding member by its own name alone, so an instance method dispatches to a same-named CLASS method of a subclass (and the reverse). Ruby names the two apart (Class#method, Class.method); the linker does not look.\n\nSEEN. chatwoot, after the member-name fix of PR #2337 made the names of namespaced class methods readable: a new dispatches_to edge Crm::BaseProcessorService#crm_name -> Crm::Leadsquared::ProcessorService.crm_name (the base class has both #crm_name and .crm_name; the edge from .crm_name to the subclass's .crm_name is also there and is right). 1 of the 10 dispatches_to edges that PR added on chatwoot. For classes without a namespace the same pairing existed before the PR; not counted.\n\nASK. Where the language's member names say instance or class (Ruby # and .), dispatch only between members of the same sort. Count the existing edges of that shape on a Ruby corpus first.\n\nFound during the WI-rasok work (PR #2337, 2026-11-22). Evidence: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rasok_11222026/RESULTS.md."  # e7af
    fields: {}  # e7af
